\section{Other Lévy processes}{

There are other Lévy processes. \code{DegradSim} does not simulate them, except
the Poisson process, given by \code{\link[=sim_cpoisson]{sim_cpoisson()}} with jumps equal to 1:
\itemize{
\item the \strong{Poisson process}, increments \eqn{Poisson(\lambda \Delta t)}
(Hoyle, 2010, section 2.11);
\item the \strong{Cauchy process}, increments \eqn{Cauchy(0, \gamma \Delta t)}, of
infinite mean (Hoyle, 2010, section 2.9);
\item the \strong{\eqn{\alpha}-stable processes}, of infinite variance for
\eqn{\alpha < 2}; for \eqn{\alpha < 1}, the stable subordinator is
increasing (Samorodnitsky and Taqqu, 1994; exact simulation by Chambers,
Mallows and Stuck, 1976);
\item the \strong{generalized hyperbolic (GH) processes}, whose distribution at time
1 is generalized hyperbolic (Barndorff-Nielsen, 1977); the NIG and the
variance-gamma processes are special or limiting cases;
\item the \strong{tempered stable processes}, stable processes whose large jumps are
tempered by an exponential factor (Koponen, 1995; Rosiński, 2007); the
gamma and inverse Gaussian processes are tempered stable subordinators;
\item the \strong{Lévy jump-diffusion processes}, a Brownian motion with drift plus
a compound Poisson process, with normal jumps (Merton, 1976) or double
exponential jumps (Kou, 2002);
\item the \strong{CGMY process} (Carr, Geman, Madan and Yor, 2002), a tempered
stable process that contains the variance-gamma process as a special
case.
}
}

\section{Time-scaled Lévy processes}{

\strong{Time-scaling function.} Let \eqn{A : [0, +\infty[ \to [0, +\infty[} be
a non-decreasing function, right-continuous with left limits, with
\eqn{A(0) = 0} and \eqn{A(t) \to +\infty} as \eqn{t \to +\infty} (a
time-scaling function, Cha and Mercier, 2019, Example 1), and let \eqn{Y}
be a Lévy process. The process
\deqn{X_t = Y_{A(t)}, \qquad t \ge 0,}
is a time-scaled Lévy process (Kahle et al., 2016, p. xix-xx; Cha and
Mercier, 2019, section 2): \eqn{X_0 = 0}, its increments are independent
and \eqn{X_t - X_s} has the distribution of \eqn{Y_{A(t) - A(s)}}. Its
increments are not stationary when \eqn{A} is not linear: \eqn{X} is not a
Lévy process, but a non-homogeneous Lévy process. When \eqn{A} is
continuous, \eqn{X} is also stochastically continuous: it is an additive
process in the sense of Sato (2001, p. 3), that is a process satisfying
the conditions 1, 2, 4 and 5 above.

\strong{The time-scaling function in the package.} \eqn{A} is given as the
argument \code{A} of \code{\link[=sim_gamma]{sim_gamma()}}, \code{\link[=sim_wiener]{sim_wiener()}} and \code{\link[=sim_ig]{sim_ig()}}, a vectorized R
function of \code{t} (\code{A(t)} returns one value per time), for instance
\code{function(t) 2 * t^1.5}. Each of these functions checks that
\eqn{A(0) = 0} and that \eqn{A} is finite and non-decreasing at the
inspection times, and raises an error otherwise. The conditions
"right-continuous with left limits" and \eqn{A(t) \to +\infty} cannot be
checked on a finite set of times: they are left to the user. If \eqn{A} is
constant between two inspection times, the increment of \eqn{X} is 0.

\strong{Identifiability.} One parameter of \eqn{Y} (\eqn{a} for the gamma
process, \eqn{\mu} for the Wiener and inverse Gaussian processes) is set to
1 and contained in \eqn{A}. With \eqn{\Delta A = A(t) - A(s)}:\tabular{lll}{
   Function \tab Parameters \tab \eqn{X_t - X_s} \cr
   \code{\link[=sim_gamma]{sim_gamma()}} \tab \code{b}, \code{A} \tab \eqn{\Gamma(\Delta A, b)} \cr
   \code{\link[=sim_wiener]{sim_wiener()}} \tab \code{sigma}, \code{A} \tab \eqn{\mathcal{N}(\Delta A, \sigma^2 \Delta A)} \cr
   \code{\link[=sim_ig]{sim_ig()}} \tab \code{eta}, \code{A} \tab \eqn{IG(\Delta A, \eta \Delta A^2)} \cr
}


The time-scaled Wiener process is \eqn{X_t = A(t) + \sigma B_{A(t)}}, with
\eqn{B} a standard Brownian motion.

\strong{Linear time-scaling function.} With \eqn{A(t) = a t}, the time-scaled
gamma process is the gamma process with shape \eqn{a}. With
\eqn{A(t) = \mu t}, the time-scaled Wiener process
\eqn{\mu t + \sigma B_{\mu t}} has the distribution of
\eqn{\mu t + \sigma \sqrt{\mu}\, B_t}, since \eqn{(B_{\mu t})_{t \ge 0}}
has the distribution of \eqn{(\sqrt{\mu}\, B_t)_{t \ge 0}}: it is the
Wiener process with drift \eqn{\mu} and diffusion coefficient
\eqn{\sigma \sqrt{\mu}}. In the same way, with \eqn{A(t) = \mu t}, the
increments of the time-scaled inverse Gaussian process are
\eqn{IG(\mu \Delta t, \eta \mu^2 \Delta t^2)}: it is the inverse Gaussian
process with parameters \eqn{\mu} and \eqn{\eta \mu^2}.
}

\section{Getting started}{

A simulation always follows the same three steps.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Choose the inspection times} \code{t}. Three forms are accepted by every
\code{sim_} function:
\itemize{
\item a numeric vector, for instance
\code{inspection_times("regular", n = 200, tmax = 10)$time} (200 regular
inspections, step 0.05): all the trajectories share these times, and
their number is given by \code{n_traj};
\item a list of numeric vectors, one per trajectory, for instance
\code{list(c(0, 1, 3), c(0, 2, 4, 6))};
\item the data frame returned by \code{\link[=inspection_times]{inspection_times()}}, which gives one plan
per trajectory, for instance with the pattern \code{"increasing"} for
trajectories inspected more and more often (\code{n_traj} is then ignored).
}

The times of each trajectory must be finite, start at 0 and be
strictly increasing, with at least two times (0 and one inspection).
These conditions are checked by every \code{sim_} function, also for the
result of \code{\link[=inspection_times]{inspection_times()}}, and an error explains which condition
fails.
\item \strong{Simulate} with the function of the chosen process and its
parameters, for instance \code{sim_gamma(t, a = 2, b = 3)} for a gamma
process. For a time-scaled Lévy process, give the time-scaling function
\code{A} (see the section Time-scaled Lévy processes) instead of the fixed
parameter, for instance \code{sim_gamma(t, b = 3, A = function(t) 2 * t^1.5)}.
\item \strong{Store and use the result} \code{x}, an object of class \code{DegradSim}. Its
form depends on the times:
\itemize{
\item \strong{same times for all the trajectories}: a matrix with the column \code{t}
(inspection times, starting at 0) and one column per trajectory, \code{X1},
..., \code{Xn} (levels, equal to 0 at \code{t = 0}). The levels of the
\eqn{k}-th trajectory are the column \code{x[, "Xk"]}, which is also the
column number \eqn{k + 1}, \code{x[, k + 1]};
\item \strong{different times}: a list with one data frame per trajectory. The
data frame \code{x[[k]]} of the \eqn{k}-th trajectory has two columns: \code{t}
(its inspection times) and \code{X} (its levels). A column can be taken by
its name or by its position: \code{x[[k]]$t} is the same as \code{x[[k]][, 1]}
(first column), and \code{x[[k]]$X} is the same as \code{x[[k]][, 2]} (second
column).
}

In both cases, \code{x} prints the model, the parameters, the time-scaling
function if any and the first simulated values; \code{traj_df(x)} gives all
the data in one data frame (\code{id}, \code{t}, \code{X}, and \code{dt}, \code{dX} with
\code{increments = TRUE}) and \code{plot_traj(x)} plots the trajectories. See the
examples below.
}

Use \code{set.seed()} before a simulation to make it reproducible.

Other functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=inspection_times]{inspection_times()}}: inspection times following a chosen pattern
(regular, increasing, decreasing, or alternating between frequent and
rare inspections);
\item \code{\link[=plot_plan]{plot_plan()}}: plot of the inspection times, one row per trajectory
(\code{ggplot2});
\item \code{\link[=plot_traj]{plot_traj()}}: plot of the simulated trajectories (\code{ggplot2});
\item \code{\link[=traj_df]{traj_df()}}: simulated data as a data frame.
}
}

\section{Exact simulation at the inspection times (random walk approximation)}{

All the simulated processes have independent increments with known
distributions. The values at the inspection times
\eqn{0 = t_0 < t_1 < \dots < t_n} are therefore simulated by the random walk
approximation (RWA) of Kahle et al. (2016, algorithms 1.7 and 2.32):
independent increments \eqn{\Delta X_i} with the distribution of
\eqn{X_{t_i} - X_{t_{i-1}}} are drawn, then \eqn{X_{t_0} = 0} and
\eqn{X_{t_i} = \Delta X_1 + \dots + \Delta X_i}. The vector
\eqn{(X_{t_1}, \dots, X_{t_n})} has exactly the distribution of the process
at the inspection times; the name "approximation" refers only to the path
between two inspection times, which is not simulated. Algorithm 2.32 is
written for the gamma process, but the same scheme applies to every process
with independent increments. The way each increment is drawn is given in
the help page of each simulation function.
}

\examples{
# 1. Inspection times: 10 trajectories, inspected more and more often
plan <- inspection_times("increasing", n = 20, n_traj = 10, tmax = 100)
plot_plan(plan)

# 2. Simulation of a gamma process (a Lévy process)
set.seed(1)
x <- sim_gamma(plan, a = 0.2, b = 1)

# 3. Same times for all the trajectories: a matrix (column t, then X1, ..., X10)
x
x[, "t"]      # inspection times
x[, "X1"]     # levels of trajectory 1
x[, 2]        # the same column, by its position
head(traj_df(x, increments = TRUE))
plot_traj(x)

# 4. Time-scaled gamma process with A(t) = 0.02 t^1.5
y <- sim_gamma(plan, b = 1, A = function(t) 0.02 * t^1.5)
y
plot_traj(y, "Time-scaled gamma process")

# 5. Different inspection times: a list of data frames (t, X)
z <- sim_gamma(list(c(0, 1, 3, 6), c(0, 2, 4, 6, 8)), a = 2, b = 3)
z[[2]]        # trajectory 2: data frame with columns t and X
z[[2]]$t      # its inspection times, the same as z[[2]][, 1]
z[[2]]$X      # its levels, the same as z[[2]][, 2]
z[[2]][, 2]
}
